trace.content:
[THOUGHT] Exposure history is unrevealing for Lyme, zoonoses, or familial autoimmunity. With chief complaint (left knee pain + decreased use), insidious non-traumatic onset, absence of systemic symptoms or overt joint inflammation, and no red flags in past history or exposures, the next critical step is targeted physical examination — especially to assess for subtle signs not reported by caregiver: gait observation (antalgic vs. Trendelenburg), knee inspection (effusion, erythema, atrophy), range of motion (painful arc, flexion contracture), patellar compression, tibial tubercle tenderness, and — crucially — hip examination (internal rotation, log roll, FABER test) to rule out referred pain from hip pathology (e.g., Perthes, SCFE, septic hip), which commonly presents as knee pain in children.

[TEST] Perform a focused musculoskeletal physical examination including:  
- Gait assessment (observe for limp, toe-walking, or Trendelenburg sign);  
- Inspection and palpation of the left knee (for swelling, warmth, effusion, focal tenderness including patella, tibial tubercle, joint line);  
- Assessment of left hip range of motion (especially internal rotation and log roll test) and comparison with right hip;  
- Thomas test to screen for hip flexion contracture.

